WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
How does a sugar added to a protein inside its ER end up outside the cell?

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.800
No vesicle ever carries it across a membrane.

3
00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:15.873
Every membrane here is two sheets of lipid, so it has two faces.

4
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:21.753
One face always touches the cytosol, the fluid around the organelles.

5
00:00:22.913 --> 00:00:27.913
The other face touches either the outside of the cell, or a lumen, the space inside

6
00:00:27.913 --> 00:00:28.913
an organelle.

7
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:32.537
Every space touching a cytosol face is gold.

8
00:00:33.857 --> 00:00:36.817
Every lumen on these routes, and the outside, is teal.

9
00:00:38.097 --> 00:00:41.917
On these routes, each membrane has one gold face and one teal face.

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.620
A patch of the plasma membrane dimples inward and pinches off as a sac.

11
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.660
Predict: what fills the sac, and which face touches the cytosol?

12
00:00:58.183 --> 00:01:01.823
It holds a gulp of outside fluid, so its lumen is teal.

13
00:01:02.963 --> 00:01:05.243
The gold face still touches the cytosol.

14
00:01:06.523 --> 00:01:07.763
Nothing crossed a membrane.

15
00:01:09.063 --> 00:01:10.203
It only changed shape.

16
00:01:11.217 --> 00:01:17.737
Here is the rule. On these routes, lumens count as outside because budding and

17
00:01:17.737 --> 00:01:20.037
fusing connect them without crossing a membrane.

18
00:01:21.217 --> 00:01:23.337
So where does a protein get its direction?

19
00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:28.157
It is normally set once, at the ER, as the chain is made.

20
00:01:29.357 --> 00:01:32.777
A channel called the translocon threads one end into the lumen.

21
00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:38.970
Here the N terminus goes into the lumen, and the C terminus stays in the cytosol.

22
00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:46.070
That is called type I. In the lumen, an enzyme adds the sugar to the N-terminal

23
00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.070
region.

24
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.740
A vesicle buds from the ER and fuses with the Golgi.

25
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.560
Its lumen joins first the ER lumen, then the Golgi lumen.

26
00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.360
The sugar never leaves teal.

27
00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:08.053
A secretory vesicle then fuses with the plasma membrane.

28
00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:11.053
Its lumen opens to the outside.

29
00:02:12.293 --> 00:02:14.953
The sugar now faces the fluid around the cell.

30
00:02:16.153 --> 00:02:18.033
It rode inside a lumen the whole way.

31
00:02:19.747 --> 00:02:22.087
Endocytosis can bring the protein back in.

32
00:02:23.287 --> 00:02:25.527
Now the sugar faces the endosome lumen.

33
00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:29.727
At no step did the C terminus leave the cytosol.

34
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.730
So the cell chooses a protein's sides once, usually at the ER.

35
00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:41.190
After that, traffic only carries it from lumen to lumen.

36
00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:47.390
Secretory and endocytic lumens count as outside; traffic never flips proteins.
